imedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Brodten%2C_23570_L%C3%BCbeck%2C_Germany_-_panoramio_%286%29.jpg/500px-Brodten%2C_23570_L%C3%BCbeck%2C_Germany_-_panoramio_%286%29.jpg","caption":"Brodten","author":"Mark Voigt","licence":"CC BY 3.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brodten,_23570_L%C3%BCbeck,_Germany_-_panoramio_(6).jpg","w":1815,"h":1200,"ok_print":true}],"intro":{"lead":"Brick-gothic Hanseatic capital, marzipan, Holstentor gate","body":"The Hanseatic City of Lübeck (Hansestadt Lübeck) is one of the major German ports on the Baltic Sea and the second-largest city in Schleswig-Holstein (about 223 000 inhabitants as of 2024), situated at the mouth of the river Trave (hence the name of its port suburb Travemünde). The city has been an important port since the 12th century. Lübeck and nearby Hamburg founded what became the powerful Hanseatic League of ports and trading towns. History also has a sweeter side for Lübeck - it is globally known for the finest marzipan.","grounding":[{"source":"wikivoyage","title":"Lübeck","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/L%C3%BCbeck","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"short":"Lubeck is a brick gothic port on the Baltic in Germany, still entered through the old Holstentor gate. The Buddenbrookhaus and the Heiligen-Geist-Hospital fill out 10 hours of sights. June to August is the best time.","short_src":"rewrite","facts":{"population":212207,"visit_h":9.6}},"highlights":[{"id":"hl-holstentor","name":"Holstentor","kind":"Gate","lat":53.8662,"lon":10.6797,"dist_km":0.5,"rank_score":11.13,"resolved_by":"catalogue"},{"id":"hl-st-mary-s-church-lubeck","name":"St. Mary's Church Lübeck","kind":"Church","lat":53.8677,"lon":10.685,"dist_km":0.3,"rank_score":9.58,"resolved_by":"catalogue","image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Aerial_image_of_St._Mary%27s_Church%2C_L%C3%BCbeck_%28view_from_the_west%29.jpg/960px-Aerial_image_of_St._Mary%27s